  "executive_summary": "This study addresses the critical challenge of sample instability in scanning tunneling microscopy (STM) measurements of functionalized carbon nanotubes (CNTs). Functional groups on CNT surfaces reduce contact area with substrates, allowing the STM tip to displace or 'clean out' the nanotubes during scanning, which inhibits reliable atomic-resolution imaging and spectroscopy. The authors propose a simple immobilization technique: incorporating functionalized multiwalled carbon nanotubes (MWCNTs) into a few-layer graphene (FLG) composite, effectively creating a buckypaper. This clamping mechanism restricts tube movement, enabling reproducible Current Imaging Tunneling Spectroscopy (CITS) measurements. The MWCNTs were functionalized via a three-step chemical process involving acid oxidation, conversion to acid chlorides, and reaction with diaminopropane to create covalent cross-links. Using this stable composite, the researchers acquired energy-resolved tunneling conductance maps (dI/dV) at various bias voltages. The results reveal distinct electronic regions on the nanotubes: graphitic regions with symmetric dI/dV curves indicating p-doping (Fermi level shift ~0.25 eV) due to acid adsorption; low-conductance regions corresponding to heavily functionalized or defective sites where the sp2 lattice is destroyed; and asymmetric dI/dV regions suggesting local charge transfer or impurity states. Crucially, atomic-resolution STM images show that functionalization and defect creation preferentially follow the zigzag crystallographic direction of the nanotube circumference. This work demonstrates that CITS on immobilized CNTs provides superior insight into local doping, charge transfer, and crystallographic dependence of functionalization compared to topography alone, opening avenues for in situ studies of gas sensing mechanisms.",
